+# Narrowing NG-9: Directory Filtering Is Not Search
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declared search for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian out of scope, leaving only owners searchable. The reason on record was that owner search already demonstrates paged prefix search once, and repeating that pattern elsewhere adds surface without teaching anything new.
+
+A request has arrived to narrow the veterinarian directory to a single specialty. This is not the free-text, prefix-matched search NG-9 declined. It selects on a whole attribute the directory already displays, against a small fixed set of specialties, and returns the matching rows unchanged. The question is whether NG-9 still covers it.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Hold NG-9 whole** — treat any narrowing of any list as "search" and decline it. Keeps one bright line, but conflates two different interactions: free-text prefix search over an open value space versus selecting one value from a closed set the page already shows.
+2. **Narrow NG-9** — keep free-text search for pets, visits, and veterinarians out, and admit filtering the veterinarian directory by a specialty it already displays. Draws the line at the interaction that carries the teaching cost (a second paged prefix-search implementation), not at every act of narrowing a list.
+
+## Decision
+
+We narrow NG-9 (Option 2). Free-text veterinarian search stays out of scope. Filtering the veterinarian directory to a single specialty it already shows is in scope, recorded as REQ-VET-004. A filter over a closed, already-displayed attribute is a different interaction from prefix search over an open value space; it does not repeat the pattern NG-9 exists to avoid duplicating.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Positive: the directory gains a genuinely new interaction to demonstrate — selection over a closed set — without a second free-text search implementation. The filter and search concepts are now distinguished in the requirement prose, which heads off drift toward re-implementing owner-style prefix matching on the directory.
+
+Negative: NG-9 is no longer a single bright line ("no search anywhere but owners"); a reader must hold the filter-versus-search distinction. The narrowing also invites the same question for other lists (pets by type, visits by date), which stay out until requested.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9
+
+## References
+
+- [PRD: NG-9](../prd.md#non-goals) and [REQ-VET-004](../prd.md#req-vet-004)
