+**Veterinarian directory filtering.** Both vet routes accept an optional `specialty` query parameter (REQ-VET-004). When it is present and non-blank, the directory is narrowed to veterinarians holding that specialty; the match is whole-name and case-insensitive — deliberately not the prefix match of owner last-name search. The narrowing is applied in application code over the cached full vet collection rather than in a database query, so it matches identically across all three vendors; owner search, which delegates matching to the database, diverges on PostgreSQL (see [Known Defects](#known-defects)). The HTML route paginates the filtered set; a blank or absent parameter yields the unfiltered directory unchanged. The machine-readable route (REQ-VET-003) returns the filtered collection through the `Vets` wrapper.
+
 ### Persistence
 
 The relational schema is not generated from the JPA mapping. `spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto` is set to `none`, and the schema is created by the per-vendor `schema.sql` scripts under `src/main/resources/db/`, with seed rows from the matching `data.sql`. The domain classes carry the JPA mapping annotations directly — there is no separate persistence model and no mapper between them. Under `architecture-principles.md` this is the sanctioned direct-mapping case: the project owns both the model and the schema, and the stored shape tracks the model closely.
